Hepatocellular Carcinoma
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Palpable irregular hepatomegaly, Rapidly worsening ascites/weight loss, Elevated AFP, Hepatic mass with arterial enhancement/washout

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-035
Reason for Visit: Rapid, severe worsening of abdominal swelling over the past week, accompanied by profound weakness, total loss of appetite, and a deep, continuous ache in the upper right stomach.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient, adult offspring.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a rapid, clinically significant worsening of pre-existing ascites, profound cachexia, and new-onset continuous right upper quadrant pain in a patient with a known history of chronic liver disease. This presentation is highly suspicious for acute decompensation driven by an expanding hepatocellular carcinoma or portal vein thrombosis. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly necessary to rule out a ruptured tumor or impending respiratory failure from tense ascites, and to expedite direct hospital admission prior to standard consulting hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My belly has completely blown up in just a few days, I've lost all the flesh on my arms, and there is a constant, deep throbbing ache under my right ribs."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports an insidious onset of dull, continuous right upper quadrant abdominal pain and discomfort over the past 2 months, which has become severe and constant over the last week. Reports unintentional, significant weight loss (10kg) and severe anorexia, combined with early satiety due to a feeling of massive abdominal fullness. Notably, reports a rapid, clinically significant worsening of pre-existing mild ascites over the past 7 days. Reports severe generalized fatigue, profound weakness, and systemic malaise. Known history of established cirrhosis secondary to chronic hepatitis C infection.
Functional Status: Bedbound, cachectic, highly distressed by abdominal distension and pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of variceal bleeding or tumor rupture; unable to maintain oral intake.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 | HR: 102 | Temp: 37.1 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 96% | Wt: 78 Kg (Cachectic but with massive ascites)
General: Patient appears emaciated in the face and limbs, chronically ill, and jaundiced.
Physical Exam: Marked scleral icterus and cutaneous jaundice are present. Abdomen is massively distended and tense, with shifting dullness indicating gross, worsening ascites. Deep palpation of the right upper quadrant, despite the fluid, reveals a distinctly hard, irregular, and markedly enlarged liver edge extending 5 cm below the costal margin. A faint hepatic bruit is auscultated over the right upper quadrant. The presentation explicitly demonstrates palpable irregular hepatomegaly and rapidly worsening ascites/weight loss; markedly elevated AFP and a characteristic hypervascular solid mass on imaging are definitively expected.
Environmental Observations: Multiple untouched nutritional supplement drinks next to the bed.
Medication Reconciliation: Compliant with diuretics (Spironolactone/Furosemide) with zero efficacy against the rapidly accumulating fluid.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Hepatocellular Carcinoma - Status: Exacerbated (Rapid clinical decompensation and tense ascites)
Differentials:

Spontaneous Bacterial Peritonitis (SBP) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of high fever and severe, diffuse rebound peritonism, and the prominent presence of a hard, irregular, palpable mass and profound cachexia pointing strongly toward an advancing malignancy as the driver of decompensation.

Portal Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The explicitly palpable hard, irregular liver edge and hepatic bruit, though a malignant portal vein thrombosis often directly accompanies advancing hepatocellular carcinoma and requires imaging to exclude.

Simple Cirrhotic Decompensation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The rapid, refractory nature of the ascites accumulating despite high-dose diuretics, combined with profound weight loss and severe, localized right upper quadrant pain indicating an active, space-occupying lesion.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Requires intensive medical stabilization and palliative planning)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the hallmark 'red flag' clinical triad for hepatocellular carcinoma in the setting of known cirrhosis: rapidly refractory ascites, profound cachexia, and a new, hard, irregular hepatomegaly. The acute failure of previously effective diuretic therapy and the onset of continuous pain signify a critical acceleration of the disease process, potentially involving malignant portal vein invasion or impending capsular rupture. An urgent, out-of-hours home assessment was mandatory to physically confirm the mass, evaluate the severity of the respiratory compromise caused by the tense ascites, and arrange an immediate, direct hospital admission for urgent diagnostic imaging, therapeutic paracentesis, and multidisciplinary oncological/palliative evaluation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered IM Buprenorphine 0.3mg to manage the severe, continuous hepatic pain. Arranged direct admission to the acute medical ward via ambulance for therapeutic large-volume paracentesis, urgent multiphase contrast-enhanced CT, and specialist oncology review.
Positive Behavioral Change: Keep your upper body elevated with several pillows while waiting for the ambulance. This mechanically prevents the massive volume of fluid in your abdomen from pushing upward and crushing your lungs, directly easing the severe shortness of breath you are experiencing.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None currently; requires inpatient stabilization and definitive diagnosis.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Acute hospital admission required.

